DNS caching is a temporary storage mechanism that saves recent DNS lookups closer to the user so that future requests for the same domain do not need to query the global internet DNS hierarchy again.Why DNS Caching ExistsA full recursive DNS lookup requires your machine to query multiple servers (Root $\rightarrow$ TLD $\rightarrow$ Authoritative), which typically takes anywhere from 30 ms to 150 ms depending on network latency.With caching:The response is retrieved from local memory in under 1 ms.It drastically reduces upstream bandwidth and query load on root and authoritative nameservers worldwide.
